# 2.1 to 3.0: a second brain without touching the first

Melee, from release 2.1 to the Melee Extension (M0 to M6)

Unit 2, talk 1

---

## Release 2.1: fight melee too

- The plan was a pure duelist, and S7 was to cut melee
- But the MeleeRumble is 10-robot free-for-alls on 1000x1000
- 2.1 ported the 1.x melee brain into the core, and the jar dropped to Java 11 so every rumble client can load it
- It runs only while two or more opponents are alive (MELEE-1)

---

## 2.1 result: mid-field

| Melee bench (35 rounds) | Mean place of 10 | Score share |
|---|---|---|
| 9 sample bots | 1.0 | 23.0% |
| 9 established melee bots | 4.4 | 10.1% |
| 9 strong bots | 5.7 | 8.6% |

It survives reasonably, but deals about half the bullet damage of the leaders. Its melee gun did not learn.

---

## The rule: 1v1 is sacred

- `DuelIdentityTest` pins a hash of every duel source file
- Melee work that touches the duel fails the build unless the PR re-pins it and says why
- **The gate (M0, M1):** each tick decides which half drives. It fails closed to the duel on a sentry or a melee fault.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    T[Each tick] --> G{2+ opponents,<br/>no sentry, no fault?}
    G -- yes --> M[Melee brain]
    G -- no --> D[Duel brain]
```

---

## M2: sensing

- A radar that keeps spinning with four or more alive (MRADAR-1)
- Robots the engine already removed are dropped at once (MRADAR-2)
- Opponents' shots read from their energy drops
- Result: the longest scan gap fell from **526** ticks (M1) to **24** (M2). The ghost half of the gate was met.
- The 8-tick gate was not: a full radar turn alone takes 8 ticks.

---

## M3: minimum-risk movement

- 160 candidate points a tick
- Risk is opponents' energy over distance squared, doubled where Hadur would be an opponent's closest robot
- Plus virtual bullets from every recorded shot

| Measure | M2 | M3 |
|---|---|---|
| Reference survival | 31.5 | **55.0** |
| Challenge firsts of 100 | 34 | **71** |

---

## M4: a learning field gun

- One aim per opponent from a shared kd-tree of their movement
- An energy table with exact kill shots, and targeting waves that score the aim without firing
- Reference APS: 50.6 (M3) to 55.0 before review, **53.1** as merged
- A review change (scoring bullets along the route) cost about 4 APS even in a solo run (50.2), so it was reverted. Guessed paths hem Hadur in.

---

## M5: memory and the hand-off

- A melee profile per opponent beside its 1v1 profile
- When one opponent is left, its 1v1 profile and its shots in flight go to the duel
- Gate: win the hand-off duels within 5 points of a clean 1v1
- Result: **31%** (8 of 26) against **57%**. Not met, and the sample is small.
- The cold bench cannot show the profile half. It needs a warm run.

---

## M6: tuning, and an honest null

- Swept the movement weights at 10 seeds (5 seeds hold about 2 APS of noise)
- They sit at a local optimum. Without virtual bullets, APS drops 3.
- The posture used to cut power and hold fire. Removing that raised bullet damage about **15%** at the same APS, so 3.0 keeps the simpler rule (MGUN-5).

---

## Release 3.0: the numbers

| Melee, cold, 1000x1000 | 2.2 | 3.0 |
|---|---|---|
| Reference field, APS | 37.9 | **52.7 to 54.6** |
| Reference field, survival | 32.6 | 59.5 to 63.4 |
| Challenge field, firsts of 100 | 33 | **72** |

The duel is unchanged: Shadow 54.5% ± 4.5 (2.2: 57.4% ± 5.0), within the interval. The plan's last gate, reference APS 60, was **not met**.

---

## What it taught us

- Protect what works with a build check (a hash), not a promise
- Fail closed: when unsure, fall back to the proven brain
- Reverse a change that the bench says hurts, even if a reviewer suggested it
- A correction counts: the M5 report blamed long duels for skipped turns, and M6 showed they were the round-end profile write
- Ship the release and say which gate it missed

---

